# slo

`slo` renders the request SLOs of one HTTP service: a Sloth
`PrometheusServiceLevel` with an availability and a latency SLO, and a
`PrometheusRule` holding the multi-window burn-rate alerts. Install it once per
service, next to the chart that runs the workload.

## What it creates

| Object | Name | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PrometheusServiceLevel` (`sloth.slok.dev/v1`) | `<service>-slo` | SLIs and recording rules, generated by the Sloth operator |
| `PrometheusRule` (`monitoring.coreos.com/v1`) | `<service>-slo-burn` | Page and ticket burn-rate alerts with a minimum-events guard |

Both go to `namespace` (default: the release namespace). Set it when the Sloth
and rule operators watch a single namespace.

## Why the burn-rate alerts are not Sloth's

Sloth generates the SLIs and recording rules, but its own page and ticket
alerts are disabled. This chart writes the same multi-window expressions and
adds one guard Sloth has no field for: every arm fires only when its long
window saw at least `minEvents` requests. On an idle service a ratio over a
handful of requests is not a burn rate; the ticket arms (1d and 3d windows)
still catch a sustained problem on a low-traffic service.

The Sloth ids stay `<service>-availability` and `<service>-latency`, and the
alert names default to `<Service>HighErrorRate` and `<Service>HighLatency`.

## Requirements

- Sloth operator and its `PrometheusServiceLevel` CRD
- A Prometheus Operator `PrometheusRule` CRD, or an operator that converts it
  (the VictoriaMetrics operator does)
- Request metrics as a histogram: `metrics.total` (`_count`) and
  `metrics.bucket` (`_bucket`), with a status-code label

## Install

```bash
helm install checkout-slo oci://ghcr.io/duynhlab/helm-charts/slo \
  --namespace checkout \
  --set service=checkout \
  --set namespace=monitoring
```

The selector defaults to `app=<service>, namespace=<release namespace>`, so
install the release in the service's own namespace or set `selector`.

## Migrating from mop

`mop`'s `slo.*` block maps one to one: `slo.availability.objective` →
`availability.objective`, `slo.latency.*` → `latency.*`, `slo.minEvents` →
`minEvents`, `slo.namespace` → `namespace`, `slo.team` / `slo.env` →
`labels.team` / `labels.env`. The object names differ (`<service>-slo`,
`<service>-slo-burn`) so both releases can exist during a cutover without a
Helm ownership conflict; the Sloth ids, recording rules and alert names are the
same.

